                                    "text": "%"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-69",
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 410.0, 120.0, 20.0, 21.0 ],
                                    "text": "%"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-42",
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 620.0, 235.0, 21.0 ],
                                    "text": "Resonant band-pass filter in Gen container"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-64",
                                    "linecount": 6,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 640.0, 245.0, 93.0 ],
                                    "text": "To perform the filtering, we've implemented a resonant band-pass filter in a Gen container. Gen allows for creating low dsp tasks such as filters with maximum accuracy, and implementing one's own filter formulas under patching or dsp code forms."
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-26",
                                    "linecount": 10,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 460.0, 253.0, 150.0 ],
                                    "text": "To obtain stereo modulation, we need to shift the action of the modulation for one of the audio channels when the Spread parameter is engaged. To do that, we're passing the modulation signals through two triangular waveform tables. If we then shift the input phase of the triangle~ object of the right channel as we increase the value of Spread, the modulation of both channels will get more and more out of phase."
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-62",
                                    "linecount": 3,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 290.0, 260.0, 50.0 ],
                                    "text": "The Tone and Color settings allow for scaling the filter frequencies and Qs, in order to alter the timbre and resonance of the filters."
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-44",
                                    "linecount": 4,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 220.0, 250.0, 64.0 ],
                                    "text": "The vowels data is dynamically written to some audio buffers when a Voice is selected (bass, tenor, alto...). Thus each resonant filter represents one component of five \"formants\"."
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-43",
                                    "linecount": 7,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 110.0, 242.0, 107.0 ],
                                    "text": "The principle of this patch is to use a normalized modulation signal to interpolate some filter data representing the resonance of human vowels -  u, o, a, e, i - and to filter the incoming audio signal out using a set or resonant filters whose frequency, amplitude and Q are fed by the vowels data."
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-24",
                                    "linecount": 7,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 350.0, 285.0, 107.0 ],
                                    "text": "In manual mode, the interpolation is controlled via a dedicated parameter. In auto mode, the modulation is produced in response to the estimation of the amplitude envelope of the input sound (envelope follower). The Sense and Slope parameters may help setting the response of the modulation signal according to the type of input signal."
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 90.0, 70.0, 21.0 ],
                                    "text": "Description"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "background": 1,
                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "id": "obj-28",
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 690.0, 60.0, 175.0, 21.0 ],
                                    "text": "Classic stereo \"talk-wah\" effect"
                                }
                            },
